s parametrized cases is fine since each test gets a fresh... actually no \u2014 the parametrize creates the exception instances at collection time, and the same instance is reused if the test runs once. Each param case is a separate test run with its own instance from the parametrize list. Fine.\n\nBut wait: HTTPError with fp=None \u2014 `to_native(e)` \u2192 str(e) \u2192 'HTTP Error 500: Server Error'. Fine. But does constructing HTTPError with hdrs=None, fp=None cause issues? `HTTPError.__init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The 'headers' attribute exists for backwards compatibility...\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.headers = hdrs\n        else:\n            self.headers = hdrs  # hmm\n        super().__init__(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)\n```\n\nActually in py3:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"...\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The 'headers' attribute exists for backwards compatibility reasons,\n        # but it is not used by urllib.response.addinfourl\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.headers = hdrs\n        else:\n            self.headers = None\n        super().__init__(url, msg, hdrs, fp)  # addinfourl init\n```\n\nHmm, addinfourl.__init__(url, headers, fp)... In older py3 versions, HTTPError.__init__ calls `urllib.response.addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, hdrs, url, code)`? Something like that. With fp=None and hdrs=None it should still construct fine \u2014 ansible's own tests construct HTTPError like this. Actually, hmm, in some Python versions, `HTTPError(None, None, None, None, None)`... Let me be safe and pass reasonable