\n    if isinstance(obj, text_type):\n        return obj\n    elif issubclass(type(obj), binary_type):\n        return obj.decode(encoding, errors)\n    elif obj is None:\n        return None\n    else:\n        return str(obj)\n```\n\nHmm, no wait. I think I'm confusing myself. Let me just check with get_info. It's one call.\n\nOK let me also double check: in the test for error messages, I should assert the specific message. E.g.:\n\n- HTTPError \u2192 \"Received HTTP error for %s : %s\"\n- URLError \u2192 \"Failed lookup url for %s : %s\"\n- SSLValidationError \u2192 \"Error validating the server's certificate for %s: %s\"\n- ConnectionError \u2192 \"Error connecting to %s: %s\"\n\nI'll assert `expected_msg in str(excinfo.value)`.\n\nNow, about AnsibleError message: AnsibleError.__str__ returns self.message. Good.\n\nNow multiple terms test:\n\n```python\ndef test_run_multiple_terms(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    responses = {\n        'http://one/': FakeResponse(b'a\\nb'),\n        'http://two/': FakeResponse(b'c\\nd'),\n    }\n    seen = []\n    def fake_open_url(term, **kw):\n        seen.append(term)\n        return responses[term]\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup, 'open_url', fake_open_url)\n    result = lookup.run(['http://one/', 'http://two/'])\n    assert result == ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\n    assert seen == ['http://one/', 'http://two/']\n```\n\nAlso a test verifying set_options is called with variables and kwargs:\n\n```python\ndef test_run_calls_set_options(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    recorded = {}\n    def fake_set_options(var_options=None, direct=None):\n        recorded['var_options'] = var_options\n        recorded['direct'] = direct\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'set_options', fake_set_options)\n    ...\n    lookup.run(['http://x/'], variables={'foo': 'bar'}, some_kwarg=1)\n    assert recorded == {'var_options': {'foo': 'bar'}, 'direct': {'some_kwarg': 1}}\n```\n\nHmm wait, but my fixture already stubs set_options; overriding again with monkeypatch.setattr is fine (monkeypatch handles undo).\n\nHmm, but actually if I go the \"real options\" route, this test would verify set_options receives variables. Let me decide after get_info.\n\nNow \u2014 the big question: real options or stubbed? Let me make the get_info calls and see.\n\nActually, let me think about what get_info returns. It \"Returns information about a symbol.\" Probably source code + signature. Let me call it for the key symbols.\n\nCalls (all independent, one block):\n1. get_info(\"LookupBase\")\n2. get_info(\"set_options\")\n3. get_info(\"get_option\")\n4. get_info(\"SSLValidationError\")\n5. get_info(\"ConnectionError\")\n6. get_info(\"to_native\")\n\nLet me do that now.\n\nThen, based on results, finalize the test.\n\nOne more thing to consider: the fixture using monkeypatch.setattr on the instance for set_options/get_option \u2014 monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) requires the attribute to exist (it does, as class methods) and will restore afterwards. Since the instance is created fresh in the fixture and discarded after, restoration doesn't matter much, but monkeypatch is still the clean way.\n\nAlso, patching `ansible.plugins.lookup.url.open_url` via monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup, 'open_url', fake) \u2014 restores after test. Good, no state pollution.\n\nAlso `display.vvvv` \u2014 fine.\n\nNow, about using real options \u2014 let me think about what would be needed:\n\n```python\ninstance = LookupModule()\ninstance.set_options(var_options=None, direct={})\n```\n\nIf `get_option_definition()` requires `self._load_name`... For a bare LookupModule, `_load_name` is not set unless PluginBase.__init__ sets it. Let me check PluginBase via get_info too? I can add get_info(\"PluginBase\"). Hmm, but the tool might only know symbols from the module in question... Let me try a broad set of calls and see what comes back.\n\nActually, thinking about it more: I recall that in ansible-core, `AnsiblePlugin.get_option_definition` does:\n\n```python\n    def get_option_definition(self):\n        ''' retrieve option specification '''\n        docs = {}\n\n        for plugin_class in self.__class__.__mro__:\n            if plugin_class.__module__.startswith('ansible.plugins.'):\n                ...\n```\n\nno. OK here's another memory \u2014 ansible/plugins/__init__.py:\n\n```python\nclass AnsiblePlugin(PluginBase):\n\n    def __init__(self):\n\n        self._options = {}\n\n        super(AnsiblePlugin, self).__init__()\n\n    def get_option(self, option, hostvars=None):\n        if self._option_values is None:\n            self._option_values = {}\n\n            try:\n                definitions = self.get_option_definition()\n            except ...\n```\n\nUgh. Fine \u2014 get_info.\n\nLet me also prepare for the possibility that get_info returns nothing useful for these symbols (maybe the tool is scoped to the file). In that case, I'll go with the stubbed-options approach (Option C), which is guaranteed to work regardless of ansible internals, since it only relies on:\n- `LookupModule()` constructible (LookupBase.__init__ with defaults \u2014 should be fine)\n- patching module attribute open_url\n- stubbing instance methods\n\nActually wait, is `LookupModule()` constructible without args? `LookupBase.__init__(self, loader=None, templar=None, shared_loader_obj=None)` \u2014 yes. And `AnsiblePlugin.__init__` / `PluginBase.__init__` take no required args. So `LookupModule()` works.\n\nHmm, one more risk with Option C: none really. It's self-contained.\n\nBut hmm, \"each test is correct and indeed improves coverage\" \u2014 with Option C, all lines 190-224 get executed across the test suite:\n- 190: set_options call (stubbed but executed) \u2713\n- 192-194: ret, loop, display.vvvv \u2713\n- 195-209: try + open_url call \u2713\n- 210-211: HTTPError handler \u2713\n- 212-213: URLError handler \u2713\n- 214-215: SSLValidationError handler \u2713\n- 216-217: ConnectionError handler \u2713\n- 219-221: split_lines True branch \u2713\n- 222: else (implicit \u2014 the `else:` line itself; covered when split_lines False) \u2713\n- 223: ret.append(to_text(response.read())) \u2713\n- 224: return ret \u2713\n\nAll covered.\n\nNow, is there any branch issue? Line 219 `if self.get_option('split_lines'):` \u2014 both branches covered. The for loop at 220 \u2014 covered with \u22651 line. Should I also cover the empty-response case (loop with zero iterations)? Not required for line coverage, but could add a test with empty bytes: `FakeResponse(b'')` \u2192 `b''.splitlines()` \u2192 [] \u2192 ret == []. That covers the loop-exhausted branch. Nice to have.\n\nLet me also think: should the tests verify that `open_url` receives exactly the expected kwargs? Yes, good postcondition.\n\nNow let me also double-check `to_text` on bytes: `to_text(b'line1')` \u2192 'line1'. Good.\n\nEdge: `b'line1\\nline2\\n'.splitlines()` \u2192 [b'line1', b'line2']. Good.\n\nNow let me write the get_info calls.\n\nActually, one more consideration on the parametrized error test: the `exc` instances are created at collection time. HTTPError construction at collection time \u2014 fine.\n\nBut hmm, there's a subtlety with parametrize and mutable exception instances: if a test modifies the exception... we don't. Fine.\n\nAlso: should each error test assert that the term appears in the message? Yes: \"for http://example.com/ :\" etc. I'll assert both the prefix and the term.\n\nLet me also think about whether `AnsibleError` message formatting: `AnsibleError(\"Received HTTP error for %s : %s\" % (term, to_native(e)))`. AnsibleError stores message; str(err) returns message (AnsibleError.__str__ returns self.message). Actually AnsibleError extends Exception and its __str__:\n\n```python\nclass AnsibleError(Exception):\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.message\n```\n\nHmm, AnsibleError.__init__ does message processing (removes \"MODULE FAILURE\" etc.? no...). It stores `self.message`. And `__str__` returns `self.message`. I believe so. So `str(excinfo.value)` gives the message. Good.\n\nNow, let me also handle: what if `to_native` raises on exception objects? Then the plugin line 211 itself would raise TypeError instead of AnsibleError... but that's the plugin's real behavior; if to_native can't handle exceptions, the plugin is broken and my test would fail. Since this is real ansible code that presumably works in production, to_native must handle it. Let me verify via get_info anyway.\n\nAlright, let me make the calls.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the scope of get_info: \"Returns information about a symbol. class, function or method name, as in 'f' for function f or 'C.foo' for method foo in class C.\" So I should query like \"AnsiblePlugin.set_options\"? The tool probably has access to the codebase being tested (ansible). Let me query:\n\n- \"LookupBase\" \n- \"AnsiblePlugin.set_options\"\n- \"AnsiblePlugin.get_option\"\n- \"SSLValidationError\"\n- \"ConnectionError\"\n- \"to_native\"\n\nHmm, but \"set_options\" alone might resolve. Let me use qualified names where sensible. I'll do:\n\n1. get_info(\"LookupBase\")\n2. get_info(\"AnsiblePlugin.set_options\")\n3. get_info(\"AnsiblePlugin.get_option\")\n4. get_info(\"SSLValidationError\")\n5. get_info(\"ConnectionError\")\n6. get_info(\"to_native\")\n\nAll in one block since independent.\n\nThen finalize the test code.\n\nLet me now think about what the final test file will look like, assuming Option C (stubbed options) as fallback and possibly Option A/B if info supports it.\n\nFinal test file draft (Option C):\n\n```python\nimport io\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.errors import AnsibleError\nfrom ansible.module_utils._text import to_native\nfrom ansible.module_utils.six.moves.urllib.error import HTTPError, URLError\nfrom ansible.module_utils.urls import ConnectionError, SSLValidationError\nfrom ansible.plugins.lookup import url as url_lookup_module\nfrom ansible.plugins.lookup.url import LookupModule\n\n\nclass FakeResponse(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal file-like object standing in for what open_url returns.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self._data = data\n        self.read_count = 0\n\n    def read(self):\n        self.read_count += 1\n        return self._data\n\n\nBASE_OPTIONS = {\n    'validate_certs': True,\n    'use_proxy': True,\n    'username': 'someuser',\n    'password': 'somepass',\n    'headers': {'X-Test': '1'},\n    'force': False,\n    'timeout': 10,\n    'http_agent': 'ansible-test-agent',\n    'force_basic_auth': False,\n    'follow_redirects': 'urllib2',\n    'use_gssapi': False,\n    'unix_socket': None,\n    'ca_path': None,\n    'unredirected_headers': [],\n    'split_lines': True,\n}\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef lookup(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"A LookupModule whose option machinery is replaced by a simple dict.\"\"\"\n    options = dict(BASE_OPTIONS)\n    instance = LookupModule()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(instance, 'set_options',\n                        lambda var_options=None, direct=None: None)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(instance, 'get_option',\n                        lambda name, hostvars=None: options[name])\n    instance._test_options = options\n    return instance\n\n\ndef _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, handler):\n    \"\"\"Patch open_url in the lookup module's namespace; return recorded calls.\"\"\"\n    calls = []\n\n    def wrapper(term, **kwargs):\n        calls.append((term, kwargs))\n        return handler(term, **kwargs)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_module, 'open_url', wrapper)\n    return calls\n\n\ndef test_run_split_lines_default(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    calls = _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, lambda term, **kw: FakeResponse(b'first\\nsecond\\nthird\\n'))\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert result == ['first', 'second', 'third']\n    assert len(calls) == 1\n    term, kwargs = calls[0]\n    assert term == 'http://ansible.example.com/'\n    assert kwargs == {\n        'validate_certs': True,\n        'use_proxy': True,\n        'url_username': 'someuser',\n        'url_password': 'somepass',\n        'headers': {'X-Test': '1'},\n        'force': False,\n        'timeout': 10,\n        'http_agent': 'ansible-test-agent',\n        'force_basic_auth': False,\n        'follow_redirects': 'urllib2',\n        'use_gssapi': False,\n        'unix_socket': None,\n        'ca_path': None,\n        'unredirected_headers': [],\n    }\n\n\ndef test_run_without_split_lines(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    lookup._test_options['split_lines'] = False\n    _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, lambda term, **kw: FakeResponse(b'one\\ntwo\\n'))\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert result == ['one\\ntwo\\n']\n\n\ndef test_run_empty_response_split_lines(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, lambda term, **kw: FakeResponse(b''))\n\n    assert lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/']) == []\n\n\ndef test_run_multiple_terms(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    responses = {\n        'http://one.example.com/': FakeResponse(b'a\\nb'),\n        'http://two.example.com/': FakeResponse(b'c\\nd'),\n    }\n    calls = _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, lambda term, **kw: responses[term])\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://one.example.com/', 'http://two.example.com/'])\n\n    assert result == ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\n    assert [term for term, _ in calls] == ['http://one.example.com/', 'http://two.example.com/']\n\n\ndef test_run_passes_variables_and_kwargs_to_set_options(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    recorded = {}\n\n    def fake_set_options(var_options=None, direct=None):\n        recorded['var_options'] = var_options\n        recorded['direct'] = direct\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'set_options', fake_set_options)\n    _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, lambda term, **kw: FakeResponse(b'x'))\n\n    lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'], variables={'k': 'v'}, custom=42)\n\n    assert recorded == {'var_options': {'k': 'v'}, 'direct': {'custom': 42}}\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize('exception, expected_prefix', [\n    (HTTPError('http://ansible.example.com/', 500, 'Internal Server Error', {}, io.BytesIO(b'')),\n     'Received HTTP error for'),\n    (URLError('connection refused'), 'Failed lookup url for'),\n    (SSLValidationError('certificate verify failed'),\n     \"Error validating the server's certificate for\"),\n    (ConnectionError('timed out'), 'Error connecting to'),\n])\ndef test_run_error_handling(lookup, monkeypatch, exception, expected_prefix):\n    def raise_error(term, **kwargs):\n        raise exception\n\n    _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, raise_error)\n\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleError) as excinfo:\n        lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    message = to_native(excinfo.value)\n    assert message.startswith(expected_prefix)\n    assert 'http://ansible.example.com/' in message\n```\n\nWait, there's an issue with `test_run_error_handling` and the SSLValidationError/ConnectionError distinction: if SSLValidationError is a subclass of ConnectionError, raising SSLValidationError hits the SSLValidationError handler (line 214) \u2014 good. Raising ConnectionError hits line 216 \u2014 good. But what if SSLValidationError is NOT a subclass and ConnectionError handler... doesn't matter, each raised type is caught by its own clause as long as ordering is right. The plugin's ordering handles both cases correctly.\n\nBut one more subtlety: `URLError` \u2014 in Python 3, `HTTPError` is a subclass of `URLError`. We raise plain URLError \u2192 caught by URLError clause. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: `ansible.module_utils.urls.ConnectionError` \u2014 wait, does ansible.module_utils.urls define ConnectionError? The import in the plugin is `from ansible.module_utils.urls import open_url, ConnectionError, SSLValidationError`. Yes, ansible/module_utils/urls.py defines:\n\n```python\nclass ConnectionError(Exception):\n    pass\n\n\nclass SSLValidationError(ConnectionError):\n    pass\n```\n\nI'm fairly confident. get_i