.\n\nNow the fixture factory:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture\ndef make_lookup(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"Factory building a LookupModule whose option machinery is backed by a\n    plain dict (fresh per instance, so tests cannot pollute each other).\"\"\"\n\n    def create_lookup(**option_overrides):\n        options = dict(BASE_OPTIONS)\n        options.update(option_overrides)\n        instance = LookupModule()\n        monkeypatch.setattr(\n            instance, 'set_options',\n            lambda var_options=None, direct=None: None)\n        monkeypatch.setattr(\n            instance, 'get_option',\n            lambda name, hostvars=None: options[name])\n        return instance\n\n    return create_lookup\n```\n\nAnd the open_url patch helper:\n\n```python\ndef _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, handler):\n    \"\"\"Replace open_url in the lookup module namespace; return recorded calls.\"\"\"\n    calls = []\n\n    def record_and_handle(term, **kwargs):\n        calls.append((term, kwargs))\n        return handler(term, **kwargs)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_module, 'open_url', record_and_handle)\n    return calls\n```\n\nTests:\n\n```python\ndef test_run_split_lines_by_default(make_lookup, monkeypatch):\n    lookup = make_lookup()\n    calls = _patch_open_url(\n        monkeypatch,\n        lambda term, **kwargs: FakeResponse('h\u00e9llo\\nw\u00f6rld\\n'.encode('utf-8')))\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert result == ['h\u00e9llo', 'w\u00f6rld']\n    assert len(calls) == 1\n    term, kwargs = calls[0]\n    assert term == 'http://ansible.example.com/'\n    assert kwargs == {\n        'validate_certs': True,\n        'use_proxy': True,\n        'url_username': 'someuser',\n        'url_password': 'somepass',\n        'headers': {'X-Test': '1'},\n        'force': False,\n        'timeout': 10,\n        'http_agent': 'ansible-test-agent',\n        'force_basic_auth': False,\n        'follow_redirects': 'urllib2',\n        'use_gssapi': False,\n        'unix_socket': None,\n        'ca_path': None,\n        'unredirected_headers': [],\n    }\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_run_without_split_lines_returns_whole_body(make_lookup, monkeypatch):\n    lookup = make_lookup(split_lines=False)\n    _patch_open_url(\n        monkeypatch,\n        lambda term, **kwargs: FakeResponse('h\u00e9llo w\u00f6rld\\n'.encode('utf-8')))\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert result == ['h\u00e9llo w\u00f6rld\\n']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_run_empty_response_with_split_lines(make_lookup, monkeypatch):\n    lookup = make_lookup()\n    _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, lambda term, **kwargs: FakeResponse(b''))\n\n    assert lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/']) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_run_multiple_terms(make_lookup, monkeypatch):\n    lookup = make_lookup()\n    responses = {\n        'http://one.example.com/': FakeResponse(b'a\\nb'),\n        'http://two.example.com/': FakeResponse(b'c\\nd'),\n    }\n    calls = _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, lambda term, **kwargs: responses[term])\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://one.example.com/', 'http://two.example.com/'])\n\n    assert result == ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\n    assert [term for term, _kwargs in calls] == [\n        'http://one.example.com/', 'http://two.example.com/']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_run_forwards_variables_and_kwargs_to_set_options(make_lookup, monkeypatch):\n    lookup = make_lookup()\n    recorded = {}\n\n    def recording_set_options(var_options=None, direct=None):\n        recorded['var_options'] = var_options\n        recorded['direct'] = direct\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'set_options', recording_set_options)\n    _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, lambda term, **kwargs: FakeResponse(b'x'))\n\n    lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'],\n               variables={'k': 'v'}, custom=42)\n\n    assert recorded == {'var_options': {'k': 'v'}, 'direct': {'custom': 42}}\n```\n\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize('exception, expected_prefix', [\n    (HTTPError('http://ansible.example.com/', 500, 'Internal Server Error',\n               {}, io.BytesIO(b'')),\n     'Received HTTP error for'),\n    (URLError('connection refused'), 'Failed lookup url for'),\n    (SSLValidationError('certificate verify failed'),\n     \"Error validating the server's certificate for\"),\n    (ConnectionError('timed out'), 'Error connecting to'),\n], ids=['http-error', 'url-error', 'ssl-error', 'connection-error'])\ndef test_run_translates_errors_to_ansible_errors(make_lookup, monkeypatch,\n                                                 exception, expected_prefix):\n    lookup = make_lookup()\n\n    def raise_error(term, **kwargs):\n        raise exception\n\n    _patch_open_url(monkeypatch, raise_error)\n\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleError) as excinfo:\n        lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    message = str(excinfo.value)\n    assert message.startswith(expected_prefix)\n    assert 'http://ansible.example.com/' in message\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 one concern about the parametrized exceptions being created at collection time and shared: each param case gets its own instance from the tuple, and each test run uses it once. Fine.\n\nAnother concern: `str(excinfo.value)` \u2014 AnsibleError.__str__ exists (shown in get_info, body elided but present). It returns the message. Good.\n\nAlso \u2014 should I worry that `AnsibleError.__str__` might return something decorated (like with traceback info)? `_get_extended_error` is only used in `__str__` when... hmm. Let me think. AnsibleError.__str__:\n\n```python\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._suppress_extended_error or self.obj is None ...:\n            return self.message\n        return self._get_extended_error()\n```\n\nHmm! Actually I recall:\n\n```python\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.message\n```\n\nin older versions, but newer versions:\n\n```python\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._show_content ... \n```\n\nHmm. Let me recall ansible/errors/__init__.py more carefully:\n\n```python\nclass AnsibleError(Exception):\n\n    def __init__(self, message='', obj=None, show_content=True, suppress_extended_error=False, orig_exc=None):\n        super(AnsibleError, self).__init__(message)\n        self._show_content = show_content\n        self._suppress_extended_error = suppress_extended_error\n        self.message = to_native(message)\n        self.obj = obj\n        self.orig_exc = orig_exc\n\n    @property\n    def message(self):\n        return self._message\n\n    @message.setter\n    def message(self, val):\n        self._message = val\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.message\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return self.__class__.__name__ + '(' + self.message + ')'\n```\n\nHmm, but then what is `_get_extended_error` for? It's used by... `__str__` in some versions:\n\nActually, I recall in ansible 2.9+:\n\n```python\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._suppress_extended_error or self.obj is None:\n            return self.message\n        return self._get_extended_error()\n```\n\nHmm no... Let me think. The extended error includes \"The error was: ...\" plus file/line info from obj. Since our AnsibleError is created with just a message (obj=None), even if __str__ consults obj, with obj=None it should return the plain message. Let me check the actual code:\n\nFrom ansible-core errors/__init__.py (2.12):\n\n```python\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._suppress_extended_error or self.obj is None:\n            return self.message\n        return self._get_extended_error()\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure this is right, but logically: `_get_extended_error` needs `self.obj` to extract file/line. With obj=None, extended error makes no sense. I'm fairly confident that with obj=None, str() returns the plain message. Actually wait, I now recall the actual code:\n\n```python\n    def __str__(self):\n        \"\"\"Return a string representation of the err